hadiths[7]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1094","Ahmad bin Mansour Al-Maruzi told us, Al-Nadr, meaning Ibn Shumail, informed us. Abbad bin Mansour informed us, Ikrimah bin Khaled informed us. Al-Makhzoumi, on the authority of Saeed bin Jubayr, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, said: I went to my aunt, and he mentioned some of the hadith, and said: “Then the Messenger of God rose May God’s prayers and peace be upon him, he went to the mosque, and he stood up to pray there, so I stood to his left, and he stayed for a while until the Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, found out. I wanted to pray as he prayed, so he took my forelock and pulled me until he placed me on his right, so the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed as he was upon. The night was two, two rak'ahs, and when the first dawn came, the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, got up and prayed nine rak'ahs, saying the salaam in each. He performed two rak'ahs, and performed the Witr prayer with one, which is the ninth, then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, continued until dawn was very bright, then he got up and bowed. The two rak'ahs of Fajr, then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, laid down on his side and fell asleep. Then Bilal came and mentioned the hadith in its length. Abu Bakr said: I have included the words of Ibn Abbas’s narration in the book of Al-Kabir. Abu Bakr said: In the report of Saeed bin Jubayr, there is no evidence that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, only performed the Witr prayer after the first dawn had come, before The rising of the second dawn, and the two dawns are two dawns. The first is its rising at night, and the second is the one that comes after its rising during the day, and I have dictated that The issue that I dictated to some of our companions who objected to the fact that performing the Witr prayer as a rak’ah is not permissible is the narrations that were narrated from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. May God bless him and grant him peace in the Witr prayer with three, and I explained its reasons in that place. Abu Bakr said: I do not remember any reliable information from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, regarding Qunoot in the Witr prayer, and I have clarified this issue. The reason for the narration of Ubayy ibn Ka’b on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, in the mention of Qunoot in the Witr prayer, and I explained its chains of transmission and taught about that. The point is that the mention of Qunoot in the narration of Ubayy is incorrect, given that the narration from Ubayy is also not proven in the odd string with three syllables, and it was narrated on the authority of Yazid bin Abi Maryam, on the authority of Abu Al-Hawra, on the authority of Al-Hasan bin Ali, that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, taught him a supplication to say in Qunoot Al-Witr"
"1095","It was narrated to us, it was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Rafi’, to us Yahya, meaning the son of Adam, to us Israel, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, On the authority of Abu Al-Hawra, on the authority of Al-Hasan ibn Ali, he said: “I memorized some words from the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, which he taught me, and I say them when I pray to God.” Yusuf bin Musa and Ziyad bin Ayyub praised him, saying: Waki` narrated to us, Yunus bin Abi Ishaq narrated to us, on the authority of Burayd bin Abi Maryam, on the authority of Abu Al-Hawra’, on the authority of Al-Hasan bin Ali, who said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, taught me words that I should say in the Qunut of Witr: “O God, guide me Among those whom You have guided, and be kind to me among those whom You have pardoned, and take care of me among those whom You have taken care of, and bless for me what You have given, and protect me from the evil of what You have decreed, for indeed You decree and You will not be decreed. “And He will not be humiliated by whomever you are in charge of. Blessed be our Lord and exalted are You.” This is a comprehensive hadith statement, except that Joseph said: He is not humiliated by whomever you are in charge of, but he did not mention it. The waw. Ibn Rafi’ said: You decide, and he did not mention the fa, and he said: He does not humiliate, and he did not mention the waw. Yusuf bin Musa told us, Ubayd Allah bin Musa told us, on the authority of Israel, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Burayd bin Abi Maryam, on the authority of Abu Al-Hawra’, on the authority of Al-Hasan bin Ali, so he mentioned the hadith like it. This news was narrated by Shu’bah bin Al-Hajjaj, on the authority of Burayd bin Abi Maryam, in the story of supplication, and he did not mention Qunut or Witr."
"1096","It is narrated by Bundar, it is narrated by Muhammad bin Jaafar, it is narrated by Shu’bah, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Maryam, it was narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abd al-Ala al-San’ani, it was narrated to us. Yazid bin Zurai’, narrated to us Shu’bah, on the authority of Abu Musa, narrated to us Muhammad bin Ja’far, narrated to us Shu’bah, on the authority of Burayd bin Abi Maryam, on the authority of Abu Al-Hawra’, he said: I asked Al-Hasan bin Ali: What did you remember from the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace? He said: He used to teach us this supplication: “O God, guide me among those whom You have guided,” like a Waki’ hadith about supplication, and he did not mention Qunoot or Witr. Shu'bah was better at memorizing it than a number such as Yunus bin Abi Ishaq, and Abu Ishaq does not know whether he heard this news from a post, or he fabricated it from him. Oh God, unless it is as some of our scholars claim that everything that Yunus narrated, on the authority of the one from whom his father Abu Ishaq narrated, is from what Yunus heard. With his father from whom he narrated, even if the news was proven from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, that he commanded Qunoot in the Witr prayer, or Qunut in the Witr prayer, he would not have performed Qunut in the Witr prayer. It is permissible for me to contradict the Prophet’s report, but I do not know it to be proven."
"1097","Al-Zuhri narrated, on the authority of Saeed bin Al-Musayyab, and Abu Salamah bin Abdul-Rahman, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace He only used Qunoot to supplicate for a people against another people. So if he wanted to supplicate against a people or supplicate for a people, he Ququt when he raised his head from the side. The second rak'ah of the Fajr prayer. Amr bin Ali and Muhammad bin Yahya praised him, saying: Abu Dawud, Ibrahim bin Saad, narrated to us on the authority of Al-Zuhri: Al-Ala’ bin Salih, a sheikh from the people of Kufa, narrated his prayers, on the authority of Zubaid, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Laila, that he asked him about Qunoot in Witr prayer, and he said: Al-Baraa bin Aazib told us: A year ago. Our praise is Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb. Our praise is Muhammad bin Bishr. Our praise is Al-Ala’ bin Salih. And this is Sheikh Al-Alaa bin Saleh, and they made a mistake in this word when he said: In the Witr prayer, but it is in the dawn prayer, not in the Witr prayer, so perhaps it was erased. From his book, between the fa and the jim, the fa became similar to the waw, and the jim was perhaps small, resembling the ta. Perhaps when he saw the people of his country They recite Qunoot in the Witr prayer, but their scholars do not recite the Qunut in the Witr prayer. It was imagined that the report of Al-Baraa is only from the Qunut in the Witr prayer. Salam bin Junada narrated to us, Waki’ narrated to us, on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Zubaid Al-Yami, who said: I asked Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Laila about qunut at dawn, He said: Last year. Sufyan Al-Thawri has a better memory than two hundred people like Al-Ala’ bin Salih. It was reported that Zubaid bin Abi Laila’s question was only about qunut in Fajr is not at Witr, so he informed him that it was the previous year, and he also did not mention Al-Bara’. Al-Thawri and Shu’bah, who were the imams of their time in hadith, narrated on the authority of Amr ibn Murrah, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Laila, On the authority of Al-Baraa: The Prophet recited Qunoot at dawn"
"1098","Salam bin Junada narrated to us, Waki’ narrated to us, on the authority of Sufyan, and Shu’bah, on the authority of Amr bin Murrah, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Laila, on the authority of Al-Baraa, that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: “You recited the Qunoot at dawn.”"
"1099","Bundar narrated to us, Muhammad bin Jafar narrated to us, Shu’bah narrated to us, on the authority of Amr bin Murrah, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Laila narrated to me, Al-Baraa narrated to me Ibn Aazib, that the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to “perform Qunut in the sunset and the morning prayers,” Ahmad Ibn Abdah told us, Abu Dawud, na Shu'bah, on the authority of Amr ibn Murrah informed him, saying: I heard Ibn Abi Laila narrating, on the authority of Al-Bara' ibn Aazib: that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace He, peace and blessings be upon him, used to perform Qunut in the morning and sunset prayers. This is the correct one on the authority of Al-Baraa bin Azib, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, not on what was narrated by Al-Alaa bin Salih. The highest reported report regarding qunut in the Witr prayer is from Ubayy ibn Ka’b during the reign of Umar ibn al-Khattab, authenticated, that they used to recite qunut after Half, meaning of Ramadan"
"1100","Al-Rabi’ bin Sulayman Al-Muradi told us, Abdullah bin Wahb told me, Yunus told me, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, Urwa bin Al-Zubayr told me, That Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, who was during the reign of Omar ibn al-Khattab with Abdullah ibn al-Arqam in charge of the treasury, that Omar went out one night in It was Ramadan, so Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari went out with him, and he circumambulated the mosque, and the people of the mosque were separated, and the man was praying. For himself, and the man prays, and the group prays with his prayer. Omar said: By God, I think that if we gathered these people together with one reciter, it would be more perfect. Then Omar decided to do so and ordered Ubayy ibn Ka’b to stand up for them during Ramadan, so Omar went out to them, while the people were praying with the prayer of their reciter, so Omar said: What an excellent innovation it is, and the one you sleep about is better than the one you wake up for, meaning at the end of the night, so the people used to get up at the beginning of the night, and they used to curse The infidels in the half: O God, kill the infidels who turn away from Your path and deny Your messengers and do not believe in Your promise, and create disagreement between Speak to them, cast terror into their hearts, and cast upon them Your wrath and torment, the God of truth. Then he sends blessings upon the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, and supplicates for the Muslims with whatever goodness he was able, then he would ask forgiveness for the believers. He said: And he used to say when he finished cursing the infidels, and sending his blessings upon the Prophet, And His request for forgiveness for the believing men and women, and His request: O God, You are the One we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we seek and seek, and we hope. Your mercy, our Lord, and we fear Your severe punishment. Indeed, Your punishment is coming for whomever you are hostile to. Then he says “Allahu Akbar” and falls prostrating.”"
